god spoilers follow...


Simply vanishing into a burst of flame does not mean good things are happening.

If the sacrifice is worthy, you'll see a four-leaf clover at your feet if you
are in a "prayable" state, which means your luck goes up by one.  If you have
prayed recently, and your god will be angered by your next prayer but is not
currently angry, you'll see the message "You have a hopeful feeling".   You'll
see this message until either this state times out (it does after a few
hundred turns - more if you are Hand of Elbereth/Arioch) or you have a
reconciliation.  I haven't looked at the code recently, but it appears that
the length of the timeout is shortened by "hopeful feeling" sacrifices.

If your god is angry, you'll see "feeling of inadaquacy" messages.  These will
continue until you sacrifice a BIG monster or lots of small-to-medium size
monsters.  BIG=dragon or greater - sometimes even that is not big enough.

Strangely enough, you can get weapons with any (accepted) sacrifice - I have
gotten Stormbringer with a newt corpse on more than one occasion.  However,
you can only get weapons if you would also get four-leaf clovers with
appropriate sacrifices.  It appears that the chance of getting a weapon and
the power of the weapon you get is not a function of the size of the monster
you offer up.

Sometimes you and your god can "share"; I have occasionally sacrificed
half-eaten corpses.

Suggestions I would make for new items:  rather than helms of "opposite"
alignment, I would have helms of alignment that were any of the three
alignments - perhaps to make it interesting they could be

cursed = chaotic
normal = neutral
blessed = lawful

This would be consistent with how altars work with water, and would make
holy/unholy water have another use.  They would also be harder to identify - a
cursed helm of alignment would not identify itself to a chaotic character, etc.
It would also create a situation where you could hoze yourself with that
blessed scroll of remove curse :-)

Also, a possibility might be that the gods could do something nasty to you if
a boon they gave you changed your alignment...  "Your helm glows softly amber
<More> A voice booms out 'Treacherous mortal!' <More> The vrock hits!" 
To be nicer, or if the helm is not yet identified, perhaps, "Your helm glows
softly amber <More> A voice booms out 'Thou art in my flock - I forbid this!'
<More> Your helmet is turned to dust and is blown away!"  Perhaps once this
happens you intrinsically become the alignment you were when wearing the helm
(unless you change alignment other ways or find another helm of alignment).

This seems to make more sense than the notion of "opposite" never being
neutral (in any case) or neutral always being opposed by chaos.  Neutral
should also be "more" neutral - someone instead of Elbereth should be the
special "neutral" patron, like Arioch is for chaos.  Perhaps the Grey Lords,
or Kwll (if we are to use Morcock) or a more "neutral" Vala like Aule (from
Tolkein).  Or to make things simpler your god could simply make you his/her
champion:  "A voice booms out: Seek out and destroy the enemies of
righteousness, my champion!"

